      <p>Plumbing is one of the most direct uses of Google Ads there is. Water is coming through a ceiling, someone searches, and the first plumber who answers usually gets the job. That urgency makes the channel valuable, and it also makes waste expensive, because emergency clicks are rarely cheap.</p>

      <h2>Three kinds of plumbing work, three campaigns</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Emergency work:</strong> burst pipes, major leaks, no water, overflowing toilets. High urgency, high value per call, often out of hours</li>
        <li><strong>Drains:</strong> blocked drains and sewer problems. Frequent, competitive and often a separate team or price list</li>
        <li><strong>Planned and high-ticket jobs:</strong> water heater replacement, repiping, bathroom plumbing. Fewer searches, more comparison, much higher job value</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When all three share one campaign, budget drifts to whatever is cheapest to click, which is rarely what is most profitable. Separate campaigns let each have its own bids, ads, schedule and budget.</p>

      <h2>Answer rate matters more than the ads</h2>
      <p>The most common leak in plumbing accounts is not in Google Ads at all. It is missed calls. Every unanswered call from an emergency search is paid for and lost.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Emergency campaigns scheduled only for the hours someone is on call</li>
        <li>Call recording or call outcome tracking, so missed and short calls are visible</li>
        <li>Call assets on every ad, because most people want to ring, not fill in a form</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Local Services Ads and Search together</h2>
      <p>Where Local Services Ads are available for your area, they sit above the normal results, charge per lead and show a Google verification badge. They suit emergency calls well. Search gives you control over which jobs you appear for, which matters for drains and water heaters. The two complement each other and should be measured separately. <a href="/learn/local-services-ads-vs-google-ads/">Local Services Ads vs Google Ads</a> covers the differences.</p>

      <h2>Count booked jobs, not ringing phones</h2>
      <p>A default call conversion counts any call over a set length. Suppliers, job seekers and people far outside your area all count, and bidding learns to buy more of them.</p>
      <p>I qualify calls against your booking system or job management software and feed booked jobs, with their value, back to the campaigns that produced them. Then a water heater replacement is worth more to the account than a tap washer, as it should be. <a href="/learn/why-google-ads-calls-are-not-becoming-jobs/">Why your Google Ads calls are not becoming jobs</a> explains the usual gaps.</p>

      <h2>Where plumbing budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Plumbing jobs, apprenticeships and training courses</li>
        <li>DIY searches: how to fix a leaking tap, how to unblock a sink yourself</li>
        <li>Searches for plumbing supplies, parts and merchants</li>
        <li>Clicks from outside your area, often caused by the default location setting. See <a href="/learn/google-ads-location-targeting-settings/">the location setting that wastes budget</a></li>
      </ul>
      <p>The <a href="/tools/negative-keyword-generator/">negative keyword generator</a> builds a starting list for most of these in a few minutes.</p>

      <h2>Geography by travel time</h2>
      <p>A plumber's margin depends on how far the van drives. Targeting by travel time rather than a single radius, with tighter bids on the edges of the area, keeps the work profitable. For emergency work, a smaller area often performs better than a large one, because response time is part of what the customer is buying.</p>

